1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,669
A team finds a file in the repository
that should never have been there.

2
00:00:08,709 --> 00:00:13,579
Somebody searches, finds a command that
removes a file from every commit, runs it in

3
00:00:13,619 --> 00:00:18,255
the clone on their laptop, force-pushes
main, and reports that the file is gone.

4
00:00:18,595 --> 00:00:23,472
Three things are wrong with that report.
The release tag still points at the old

5
00:00:23,512 --> 00:00:28,573
commits. The server still holds every old
object. And every colleague's clone still

6
00:00:28,613 --> 00:00:33,674
holds the complete old history, and can
push it back with an ordinary push tomorrow

7
00:00:33,714 --> 00:00:34,165
morning.

8
00:00:34,505 --> 00:00:39,465
Today you see a whole-history rewrite the
way an operator has to see it: as a change

9
00:00:39,505 --> 00:00:44,405
to every ref and to every commit ID after
the first affected commit, with a list of

10
00:00:44,445 --> 00:00:48,572
owners, an order and a verification.
The command is the smallest part.

11
00:00:48,612 --> 00:00:53,333
Keep one question in mind. After that
force-push, can the server still print the

12
00:00:53,373 --> 00:00:55,178
file? The terminal will answer.

13
00:00:57,018 --> 00:01:00,849
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:00,889 --> 00:01:05,291
Four words first. A commit is one
saved snapshot of the whole project.

15
00:01:05,331 --> 00:01:10,621
A repository is the store that holds the
commits, and a clone is a copy of it on one

16
00:01:10,661 --> 00:01:15,824
machine. A ref is a name that holds the ID
of a commit: every branch and every tag

17
00:01:15,864 --> 00:01:16,395
is a ref.

18
00:01:16,735 --> 00:01:22,156
In video 166 you learned the six steps of
a leak response, and why containment comes

19
00:01:22,196 --> 00:01:27,551
first: the credential is revoked at its
issuer, the service that accepts it, before

20
00:01:27,591 --> 00:01:33,011
anything is done to the repository. The
third step, eradicate, said "rewrite history

21
00:01:33,051 --> 00:01:36,586
only where warranted". This
video is about that clause.

22
00:01:36,926 --> 00:01:40,739
You already have the two facts that
explain everything you're about to see.

23
00:01:41,079 --> 00:01:46,139
From video 52: a commit's ID is the hash of
its content, a fingerprint computed from

24
00:01:46,179 --> 00:01:51,117
its bytes, and that content includes the
ID of its parent, the commit it was built

25
00:01:51,157 --> 00:01:54,699
on. So a commit whose parent
changes is a different commit.

26
00:01:55,039 --> 00:02:00,339
From video 79: a forced push moves refs on
the remote, here the server, and deletes

27
00:02:00,379 --> 00:02:05,680
no objects. An object is one stored unit
in Git, such as a commit or the content of

28
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:06,130
a file.

29
00:02:06,470 --> 00:02:11,559
Three things today. First, what the recommended
tool, git-filter-repo, requires and

30
00:02:11,599 --> 00:02:16,256
records, as section 21B.16 gives it from
the tool's manual and from GitHub's

31
00:02:16,296 --> 00:02:21,261
procedure. Second, the mechanics, replayed
locally with a command that ships with

32
00:02:21,301 --> 00:02:24,475
Git, so that you can watch
the refs and the objects.

33
00:02:24,515 --> 00:02:29,480
Third, what the rewrite costs, because the
costs decide whether you do it at all.

34
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:35,316
One layer note before we start. Everything
in the terminal today is Git, on a local

35
00:02:35,356 --> 00:02:40,919
bare repository that plays the server. A
bare repository holds history only, with no

36
00:02:40,959 --> 00:02:46,388
files to edit, as a server does. What GitHub
does with a rewrite is the subject of

37
00:02:46,428 --> 00:02:47,055
video 168.

38
00:02:48,895 --> 00:02:50,473
Here's what you'll walk away with.

39
00:02:50,813 --> 00:02:52,225
After this video you can:

40
00:02:52,565 --> 00:02:57,037
Say what a whole-history rewrite replaces
and why the command is the smallest part

41
00:02:57,077 --> 00:02:57,698
of the work.

42
00:02:58,038 --> 00:03:04,189
State what git-filter-repo requires and
records, as section 21B.16 gives it from the

43
00:03:04,229 --> 00:03:05,221
tool's manual.

44
00:03:05,561 --> 00:03:10,695
Follow the mechanics locally: fresh mirror
clone, all refs, new IDs, old objects

45
00:03:10,735 --> 00:03:13,153
that remain, pruning, the forced push.

46
00:03:13,493 --> 00:03:16,997
Explain what happens to signatures
and to open pull requests.

47
00:03:17,337 --> 00:03:19,733
List the costs that
decide against a rewrite.

48
00:03:21,573 --> 00:03:26,668
Why it exists. Some data stays harmful after
its credential is rotated, or can't be

49
00:03:26,708 --> 00:03:31,431
rotated at all: personal data, customer
records, proprietary model weights, a

50
00:03:31,471 --> 00:03:36,566
private key whose public half is pinned in
devices, a secret whose revocation takes

51
00:03:36,606 --> 00:03:41,639
weeks. For that data, and only for that
data, you want the repository's history to

52
00:03:41,679 --> 00:03:42,814
stop containing it.

53
00:03:43,154 --> 00:03:47,748
What it is, in one sentence. A whole-history
rewrite replaces the first affected

54
00:03:47,788 --> 00:03:52,498
commit and every descendant with new commits
that have new IDs, and the command is

55
00:03:52,538 --> 00:03:57,016
the smallest part of the work. Descendants
are the commits built on top of it.

56
00:03:57,356 --> 00:04:01,748
How: the recommended tool. The
recommended tool is git-filter-repo.

57
00:04:01,788 --> 00:04:07,305
It's a separate program and it isn't part
of Git. It isn't installed in the lab, and

58
00:04:07,345 --> 00:04:12,531
this course installs nothing. Section
21B.16 gives five facts about it from its

59
00:04:12,571 --> 00:04:16,302
manual and from GitHub's page
on removing sensitive data.

60
00:04:16,642 --> 00:04:21,909
One. It refuses to run outside a fresh
clone unless forced, because the rewrite is

61
00:04:21,949 --> 00:04:26,893
irreversible: by default it ends with
an immediate pruning of reflogs and old

62
00:04:26,933 --> 00:04:32,200
objects. A reflog is a clone's own list of
where each ref has pointed, and pruning

63
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:33,171
means deleting.

64
00:04:33,511 --> 00:04:38,319
Two. The option --sensitive-data-removal
exists since version 2.47.

65
00:04:38,359 --> 00:04:44,396
It fetches all refs first and gathers the
extra information needed to clean up other

66
00:04:44,436 --> 00:04:44,903
copies.

67
00:04:45,243 --> 00:04:50,838
Three. It records its work in .git/filter-repo/:
a file commit-map with the old and

68
00:04:50,878 --> 00:04:55,115
new ID of every commit, and the
files ref-map, changed-refs and

69
00:04:55,155 --> 00:04:56,608
first-changed-commits.

70
00:04:56,948 --> 00:05:02,394
Four. Commits get new IDs, so signatures
on commits and tags can't remain valid and

71
00:05:02,434 --> 00:05:07,946
are removed. A signature is a cryptographic
seal over the bytes of one commit or one

72
00:05:07,986 --> 00:05:11,185
annotated tag. Signed tags
become annotated tags.

73
00:05:11,525 --> 00:05:16,647
Five. By default a full rewrite removes
the origin remote, as a forcing function

74
00:05:16,687 --> 00:05:21,744
against pushing by reflex. origin is the
name under which a clone remembers the

75
00:05:21,784 --> 00:05:23,293
repository it came from.

76
00:05:23,633 --> 00:05:30,531
Read the slide as five steps: install, fresh
clone, filter by path or by text, count

77
00:05:30,571 --> 00:05:37,056
the affected pull requests, force-push
every ref. Before the push, the manual's

78
00:05:37,096 --> 00:05:43,995
verification is git log --all --name-status
-- <file> and git log -S"<string>" --all

79
00:05:44,035 --> 00:05:46,555
-p --. Both must print nothing.

80
00:05:46,895 --> 00:05:51,514
One statement here is marked unverified
in the textbook, and I say it as the

81
00:05:51,554 --> 00:05:55,620
textbook does. Whether git-filter-repo
keeps the origin remote when

82
00:05:55,660 --> 00:06:00,401
--sensitive-data-removal is used wasn't
tested for the research report of this

83
00:06:00,441 --> 00:06:05,182
course. GitHub's instructions push to
origin afterwards, and the manual says a

84
00:06:05,222 --> 00:06:09,840
default full rewrite removes it. If the
push fails for a missing remote, the

85
00:06:09,880 --> 00:06:12,721
manual's remedy is git
remote add origin <url>.

86
00:06:13,061 --> 00:06:17,860
What makes it an operation. Around those
five commands stand six facts, each with a

87
00:06:17,900 --> 00:06:18,560
consequence.

88
00:06:18,900 --> 00:06:23,767
Stay on the third and fourth rows, because
they answer two of today's objectives.

89
00:06:23,807 --> 00:06:28,614
A pull request is a GitHub object that
proposes merging one branch into another.

90
00:06:28,654 --> 00:06:33,400
Its review comments are attached to commit
IDs and lines, and the IDs no longer

91
00:06:33,440 --> 00:06:38,368
exist on the branch. So the comments detach,
and the diffs of closed pull requests

92
00:06:38,408 --> 00:06:38,732
break.

93
00:06:39,072 --> 00:06:44,019
A signature covers the commit object, the
object is replaced, so the signature can't

94
00:06:44,059 --> 00:06:49,006
be carried over. The textbook's row says
signatures are removed also on commits that

95
00:06:49,046 --> 00:06:53,934
predate the removed data. A rule that
requires signed commits therefore rejects the

96
00:06:53,974 --> 00:06:56,309
rewritten history unless it is bypassed.

97
00:06:56,649 --> 00:07:01,789
When not. Section 21B.22 lists five cases.
Don't rewrite for a credential that is

98
00:07:01,829 --> 00:07:06,521
revoked and whose provider logs show
no use: record the decision and stop.

99
00:07:06,561 --> 00:07:11,510
Don't rewrite a public repository in the
belief that it un-publishes anything.

100
00:07:11,550 --> 00:07:16,754
Don't rewrite before containment. Don't
rewrite in your working clone, because the

101
00:07:16,794 --> 00:07:21,550
tool prunes reflogs and stashes, the
work you set aside without committing.

102
00:07:21,590 --> 00:07:26,794
And don't have each colleague run the same
command: identical commands can produce

103
00:07:26,834 --> 00:07:27,690
different IDs.

104
00:07:28,030 --> 00:07:31,473
Older guides use git filter-branch
or the BFG Repo-Cleaner.

105
00:07:31,513 --> 00:07:36,197
Git's own manual says of git filter-branch
that its "use is not recommended" and

106
00:07:36,237 --> 00:07:40,920
points to git-filter-repo. GitHub's current
page documents git-filter-repo only.

107
00:07:40,960 --> 00:07:45,348
Keep that in mind for the demonstration,
because the demonstration uses git

108
00:07:45,388 --> 00:07:50,308
filter-branch, for one reason only: it ships
with Git, and the lab installs nothing.

109
00:07:50,648 --> 00:07:55,601
It isn't the recommended tool. It's enough
to show four mechanics that are the same

110
00:07:55,641 --> 00:08:00,474
whichever tool rewrites: descendants get
new IDs, tags must be rewritten too, old

111
00:08:00,514 --> 00:08:04,685
objects remain until pruned, and the
server keeps them after the push.

112
00:08:06,525 --> 00:08:11,545
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy
is the recall of a printed book to remove

113
00:08:11,585 --> 00:08:14,981
one page. You can reprint
every copy in your warehouse.

114
00:08:15,021 --> 00:08:19,104
Each reader's copy stays as it was
until that reader exchanges it.

115
00:08:19,144 --> 00:08:22,290
The page numbers after the
removed page all change.

116
00:08:22,330 --> 00:08:26,038
Every citation by page number
now points at the wrong place.

117
00:08:26,078 --> 00:08:31,285
And one reader who lends an old copy to the
library puts the page back on the shelf.

118
00:08:31,625 --> 00:08:35,321
Map it. The warehouse is the
cleanup clone and the server.

119
00:08:35,361 --> 00:08:40,088
The readers' copies are clones and
forks. The page numbers are commit IDs.

120
00:08:40,128 --> 00:08:45,113
The citations are everything that stored
an ID: issue comments, release notes,

121
00:08:45,153 --> 00:08:50,396
experiment records, deployment manifests.
The reader who lends the old copy is the

122
00:08:50,436 --> 00:08:52,328
stale clone of the next video.

123
00:08:52,668 --> 00:08:57,483
Where the analogy breaks: a reprinted book
has no memory of the old edition, and a

124
00:08:57,523 --> 00:09:02,100
Git repository does. After the rewrite
the old objects are still in the object

125
00:09:02,140 --> 00:09:05,120
database, unreachable, until
something prunes them.

126
00:09:05,160 --> 00:09:08,435
The warehouse still has the
old copies in the back room.

127
00:09:08,775 --> 00:09:13,802
So the model to carry is this. A rewrite
does three separate things, and only the

128
00:09:13,842 --> 00:09:17,242
first one is done by the
filter: it writes new commits.

129
00:09:17,282 --> 00:09:22,183
Then refs are moved to them. Then,
separately and deliberately, old objects are

130
00:09:22,223 --> 00:09:27,375
deleted. Each of the three happens per
repository. Nothing you do in one repository

131
00:09:27,415 --> 00:09:29,126
deletes anything in another.

132
00:09:30,966 --> 00:09:34,033
Watch the "before" line
turn into the "after" line.

133
00:09:34,073 --> 00:09:38,357
Eight commits on main, the fifth is
where .env was added, two tags, and

134
00:09:38,397 --> 00:09:39,454
feature/streaming.

135
00:09:39,794 --> 00:09:45,045
In the "after" line, the first four commits
are the same objects: same IDs, drawn in

136
00:09:45,085 --> 00:09:45,990
the same place.

137
00:09:46,330 --> 00:09:49,322
From the fifth commit on,
every ID is different.

138
00:09:49,662 --> 00:09:54,694
Quick quiz. The sixth, seventh and eighth
commits got new IDs as well, although

139
00:09:54,734 --> 00:10:00,087
nobody edited the changes they make. Which
field of a commit makes that unavoidable:

140
00:10:00,127 --> 00:10:04,003
A, the message, B, the parent,
or C, the author? Your answer?

141
00:10:08,843 --> 00:10:13,415
B, the parent. Each commit records its
parent's ID, and the parent was replaced.

142
00:10:13,455 --> 00:10:18,084
The words on the picture are the sentence
to remember: shared and unchanged up to

143
00:10:18,124 --> 00:10:22,119
the commit before the leak, replaced
from the first changed commit on.

144
00:10:22,459 --> 00:10:29,470
v0.1.0 stays where it was. v0.2.0 has
to move, and so does feature/streaming.

145
00:10:31,310 --> 00:10:36,458
Into the lab. The secret in this repository
is a dummy string, and it says so in its

146
00:10:36,498 --> 00:10:37,013
own text.

147
00:10:37,353 --> 00:10:42,132
Step 1: a fresh mirror clone. 🟢 SAFE:
a clone creates a new repository.

148
00:10:42,172 --> 00:10:47,697
A mirror clone has every ref of the server
as a local ref, which is what a rewrite

149
00:10:47,737 --> 00:10:48,104
needs.

150
00:10:48,444 --> 00:10:53,529
Make your prediction. How many refs will
the listing show, and of which kinds?

151
00:10:53,569 --> 00:10:54,580
Say it out loud.

152
00:10:59,420 --> 00:11:05,011
Four refs: two branches and two tags. The
tags are of type tag, so they're annotated

153
00:11:05,051 --> 00:11:10,575
tags, stored as objects of their own. Now
the last line: the commit that added .env

154
00:11:10,615 --> 00:11:15,871
is 0805fd8, "Add staging settings".
Everything from that commit on is affected.

155
00:11:15,911 --> 00:11:18,150
The four commits below it are not.

156
00:11:18,490 --> 00:11:23,771
Try it now, for thirty seconds. In the
lab shell, or in any repository you have,

157
00:11:23,811 --> 00:11:29,290
type git for-each-ref. It only reads. Each
line is one ref and the object it points

158
00:11:29,330 --> 00:11:31,286
at. Count the tags. I'll wait.

159
00:11:36,126 --> 00:11:41,347
Each line you saw is a ref, and any ref
that still points into old history keeps it

160
00:11:41,387 --> 00:11:44,961
alive. So a rewrite has to
cover them all, tags included.

161
00:11:45,001 --> 00:11:48,194
Now watch what happens when
the tags are forgotten.

162
00:11:48,534 --> 00:11:52,879
Step 2: the mistake first. Rewrite
the branches and forget the tags.

163
00:11:52,919 --> 00:11:56,940
🔴 DANGEROUS. Before running a
history filter, the five answers.

164
00:11:56,980 --> 00:12:01,970
What it changes: every affected commit
and all descendants, on every ref it is

165
00:12:02,010 --> 00:12:07,192
given. What it can destroy: signatures,
and with a wrong path argument, files you

166
00:12:07,232 --> 00:12:08,095
meant to keep.

167
00:12:08,435 --> 00:12:13,157
How to preview: git-filter-repo has
--dry-run. And you run in a fresh clone.

168
00:12:13,197 --> 00:12:17,543
How to recover: the untouched server
and other clones, until you push.

169
00:12:17,583 --> 00:12:21,741
When it is appropriate: for data
that stays harmful after rotation.

170
00:12:21,781 --> 00:12:26,315
For an unpushed mistake on a private
branch a rebase is the smaller tool.

171
00:12:26,655 --> 00:12:32,149
Predict. After the branches are rewritten,
does a scan over all refs still find the

172
00:12:32,189 --> 00:12:33,815
key? Yes or no, out loud.

173
00:12:38,655 --> 00:12:44,860
Hold on the two counts. Over the branches:
zero. Over all refs: five snapshots that

174
00:12:44,900 --> 00:12:50,954
contain the key. So the answer was yes.
The last command says why: the tag v0.2.0

175
00:12:50,994 --> 00:12:53,587
contains the first affected commit.

176
00:12:53,927 --> 00:12:59,577
The tag points at the old commit 64b9b89,
and a tag keeps its commit and all of that

177
00:12:59,617 --> 00:13:04,454
commit's ancestors alive. Anyone who
fetches the tag fetches the secret.

178
00:13:04,494 --> 00:13:09,533
One more detail from the textbook: --all
also includes the backup refs that

179
00:13:09,573 --> 00:13:12,310
filter-branch wrote under refs/original/.

180
00:13:12,650 --> 00:13:16,129
Step 3: all refs, with tags
following their commits.

181
00:13:16,469 --> 00:13:22,591
Read three things. The branches are
reported "unchanged" because the first run

182
00:13:22,631 --> 00:13:28,595
already rewrote them. v0.1.0 is unchanged
because it points before the leak.

183
00:13:28,635 --> 00:13:32,308
v0.2.0 was rewritten and
now points at 66a99cc.

184
00:13:32,648 --> 00:13:35,280
Step 4: the map of old and new IDs.

185
00:13:35,620 --> 00:13:40,055
Predict again. Of the eight commits
on main, how many have a new ID?

186
00:13:40,095 --> 00:13:41,107
Say it out loud.

187
00:13:45,947 --> 00:13:50,216
Four. The four commits below the leak
are byte for byte the same objects.

188
00:13:50,256 --> 00:13:53,522
This table is what git-filter-repo
writes to commit-map.

189
00:13:53,562 --> 00:13:58,244
In an incident, that file belongs in the
incident record, so that old IDs can be

190
00:13:58,284 --> 00:13:58,893
translated.

191
00:13:59,233 --> 00:14:04,994
Now the reasons. 0805fd8 became
0ac4257 because its tree changed.

192
00:14:05,334 --> 00:14:10,486
The three above it changed for two reasons.
Every one of their snapshots held .env

193
00:14:10,526 --> 00:14:15,362
too, so their trees changed. And each
records its parent's ID, and the parent

194
00:14:15,402 --> 00:14:18,401
changed. That second reason
is enough by itself.

195
00:14:18,741 --> 00:14:24,555
Step 5: the old objects are still there. A
rewrite adds new objects and moves refs.

196
00:14:24,595 --> 00:14:26,600
It deletes nothing by itself.

197
00:14:26,940 --> 00:14:32,119
git cat-file -t answers commit: the object
exists. git show prints the file with the

198
00:14:32,159 --> 00:14:35,598
key from it. In this clone,
after a rewrite of all refs.

199
00:14:35,938 --> 00:14:42,226
Step 6: pruning. 🔴 DANGEROUS. The five
answers for git reflog expire --expire=now

200
00:14:42,266 --> 00:14:48,553
--all followed by git gc --prune=now. What
it changes: all reflog entries and all

201
00:14:48,593 --> 00:14:55,115
unreachable objects are deleted. What it
can destroy: every commit, stash and staged

202
00:14:55,155 --> 00:14:59,490
blob that only a reflog, or
nothing at all, was keeping.

203
00:14:59,530 --> 00:15:04,568
Preview: git fsck --unreachable
--no-reflogs lists what would go.

204
00:15:04,608 --> 00:15:10,973
Recovery: none locally. When appropriate:
in a cleanup clone, and in a stale clone

205
00:15:11,013 --> 00:15:17,379
after its owner has saved their work. In
your working clone it also discards every

206
00:15:17,419 --> 00:15:17,848
stash.

207
00:15:18,188 --> 00:15:23,263
Three deliberate steps: delete the
backup refs, expire the reflogs, prune.

208
00:15:23,303 --> 00:15:29,000
Now git cat-file -t fails with "Not a valid
object name", the scan prints zero, and

209
00:15:29,040 --> 00:15:30,659
git fsck prints nothing.

210
00:15:30,999 --> 00:15:35,709
git-filter-repo performs this pruning for
you at the end of its run, which is why it

211
00:15:35,749 --> 00:15:37,122
insists on a fresh clone.

212
00:15:37,462 --> 00:15:42,784
Step 7: the forced push. 🔴 DANGEROUS. The
five answers before the command is run.

213
00:15:42,824 --> 00:15:48,344
What it changes: git push --force --mirror
sets every ref on the remote to the local

214
00:15:48,384 --> 00:15:53,905
value and deletes those the clone lacks.
What it can destroy: branches and tags that

215
00:15:53,945 --> 00:15:59,068
exist only on the remote. A branch that
a colleague pushed after you cloned is

216
00:15:59,108 --> 00:16:00,458
removed by your push.

217
00:16:00,798 --> 00:16:06,243
That's the mechanical reason for the
freeze. Preview: git push --dry-run --force

218
00:16:06,283 --> 00:16:10,974
--mirror origin. Recovery: another
clone that still has the old refs.

219
00:16:11,014 --> 00:16:16,733
On GitHub, the instruments of Chapter 13.
When appropriate: once, after a freeze, at

220
00:16:16,773 --> 00:16:22,081
the end of a verified rewrite. Every
other force push uses --force-with-lease.

221
00:16:22,421 --> 00:16:28,981
Three forced updates, each with a plus
sign: two branches and the tag v0.2.0.

222
00:16:29,021 --> 00:16:32,924
v0.1.0 isn't listed,
because it didn't change.

223
00:16:33,264 --> 00:16:37,843
Step 8: what the server holds now.
Here's the question from the opening.

224
00:16:37,883 --> 00:16:42,718
Predict before running: after this push,
can the server still print the key?

225
00:16:42,758 --> 00:16:44,001
Yes or no, out loud.

226
00:16:48,841 --> 00:16:53,811
This is the output the whole video was
built for, and it closes the question from

227
00:16:53,851 --> 00:16:57,089
the opening. No ref on the
server reaches the secret.

228
00:16:57,129 --> 00:17:02,099
The scan over all refs is clean. And git
show on the server still prints the key,

229
00:17:02,139 --> 00:17:06,243
from a commit that git fsck lists
as unreachable, with four others.

230
00:17:06,583 --> 00:17:10,625
Unreachable means that no
ref leads to it any more.

231
00:17:10,665 --> 00:17:16,068
A bare repository of your own can
be pruned with git gc --prune=now.

232
00:17:16,108 --> 00:17:20,310
On GitHub you can't run that.
Video 168 says who can.

233
00:17:20,650 --> 00:17:24,422
Read the last two columns of the
first two rows: unchanged, unchanged.

234
00:17:24,762 --> 00:17:29,649
Nothing you did in the cleanup clone touched
the remote until the push, and the push

235
00:17:29,689 --> 00:17:30,588
moved refs only.

236
00:17:32,428 --> 00:17:34,076
Five mistakes to watch for.

237
00:17:34,416 --> 00:17:39,353
Rewriting the branches and forgetting the
tags. Root cause: a tag is a ref, and any

238
00:17:39,393 --> 00:17:44,029
ref that still points into the old history
keeps the old commits and all their

239
00:17:44,069 --> 00:17:46,068
ancestors reachable and fetchable.

240
00:17:46,408 --> 00:17:51,416
Declaring the secret removed after the
force push. Root cause: a force push moves

241
00:17:51,456 --> 00:17:56,589
refs; the old objects stay on the server
as unreachable objects, and in every other

242
00:17:56,629 --> 00:17:58,770
clone and fork as ordinary history.

243
00:17:59,110 --> 00:18:03,992
Rewriting in the working clone. Root cause:
the tool finishes by expiring reflogs

244
00:18:04,032 --> 00:18:08,063
and pruning, which destroys the
local safety net, stashes included.

245
00:18:08,403 --> 00:18:13,757
Taking the mirror clone before the freeze.
Root cause: git push --force --mirror

246
00:18:13,797 --> 00:18:19,421
deletes every remote ref that the cleanup
clone does not have, so a branch pushed in

247
00:18:19,461 --> 00:18:20,702
between is removed.

248
00:18:21,042 --> 00:18:26,065
Rewriting before revoking. Root cause: the
damage happens at the issuer, where the

249
00:18:26,105 --> 00:18:31,066
key is accepted, and no change to any
repository makes a copied key stop working.

250
00:18:32,906 --> 00:18:37,937
Now, out of the lab. An ML platform team
finds that a file with customer support

251
00:18:37,977 --> 00:18:42,438
transcripts was committed to the
evaluation repository four months ago.

252
00:18:42,478 --> 00:18:47,700
This isn't a credential. Nothing can be
rotated, so the data stays harmful, and the

253
00:18:47,740 --> 00:18:50,363
textbook's criterion for a rewrite is met.

254
00:18:50,703 --> 00:18:55,364
The lead writes the plan before anyone types
a command: a freeze from a stated time,

255
00:18:55,404 --> 00:19:00,009
the rules that block force pushes and tag
updates switched off for the duration and

256
00:19:00,049 --> 00:19:04,654
an owner for switching them back on, a fresh
clone on one machine, the verification

257
00:19:04,694 --> 00:19:09,019
commands, the count of affected pull
requests for the Support request, and the

258
00:19:09,059 --> 00:19:11,537
instruction to colleagues
about their clones.

259
00:19:11,877 --> 00:19:14,984
Then comes the cost that
is specific to an ML team.

260
00:19:15,024 --> 00:19:20,105
Every experiment record, model card and
deployment manifest that stored a commit ID

261
00:19:20,145 --> 00:19:24,856
from the last four months now points at
a commit that no longer exists on the

262
00:19:24,896 --> 00:19:28,434
remote. The team doesn't try
to edit all of those records.

263
00:19:28,774 --> 00:19:33,763
It stores git-filter-repo's commit-map file
in the incident record, so that anyone

264
00:19:33,803 --> 00:19:38,669
who holds an old ID can translate it. And
because the repository required signed

265
00:19:38,709 --> 00:19:43,697
commits, the lead plans the bypass for the
rewritten history in advance, since the

266
00:19:43,737 --> 00:19:44,924
signatures are gone.

267
00:19:46,764 --> 00:19:52,096
Your turn. Do Lab 31.1, "The tabletop:
a committed secret, from report to

268
00:19:52,136 --> 00:19:56,144
prevention", in
lab-manual/m31-secret-leak-response.md.

269
00:19:56,184 --> 00:20:01,884
It runs entirely in the lab shell, with
a bare repository as the server, three

270
00:20:01,924 --> 00:20:03,798
clones and a dummy secret.

271
00:20:04,138 --> 00:20:08,947
Before you run the rewrite in the lab, write
down three predictions: which refs will

272
00:20:08,987 --> 00:20:13,565
be reported as rewritten and which as
unchanged, how many of the commits on main

273
00:20:13,605 --> 00:20:18,356
will have new IDs, and what git show of the
first affected commit will print on the

274
00:20:18,396 --> 00:20:21,473
server after the forced push.
Then run it and compare.

275
00:20:21,513 --> 00:20:24,475
The lab's questions have no
answers in the lab file.

276
00:20:24,515 --> 00:20:26,842
Attempt them before you open the answers.

277
00:20:27,182 --> 00:20:34,527
When that is done, the challenge is Exercise
31.5, Level 4, "How far did it get?",

278
00:20:34,567 --> 00:20:38,219
in exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

279
00:20:42,059 --> 00:20:45,069
Answer it out loud before
you open the answers file.

280
00:20:45,109 --> 00:20:49,994
A strong answer does four things. It starts
with the layer where the damage happens,

281
00:20:50,034 --> 00:20:54,627
the issuer, and says what revocation
achieves that no repository operation can.

282
00:20:54,667 --> 00:20:59,553
It gives the criterion for when a rewrite
is warranted at all, with examples of data

283
00:20:59,593 --> 00:21:00,843
that can't be rotated.

284
00:21:01,183 --> 00:21:06,012
It names the costs concretely: what happens
to clones, to recorded commit IDs, to

285
00:21:06,052 --> 00:21:10,160
signatures, to open pull requests,
and what the rewrite can't recall.

286
00:21:10,200 --> 00:21:14,067
And it ends with a decision that
is written down with its reason.

287
00:21:14,107 --> 00:21:19,057
An answer that describes only the filter
command has answered a different question.

288
00:21:20,897 --> 00:21:26,064
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say these sentences, in your own words.

289
00:21:26,404 --> 00:21:30,979
A whole-history rewrite replaces the first
affected commit and every descendant with

290
00:21:31,019 --> 00:21:35,210
new commits that have new IDs; the
ancestors of the first affected commit are

291
00:21:35,250 --> 00:21:35,759
untouched.

292
00:21:36,099 --> 00:21:41,189
git-filter-repo is a separate program; it
wants a fresh clone, records a commit map,

293
00:21:41,229 --> 00:21:43,754
removes signatures, and prunes at the end.

294
00:21:44,094 --> 00:21:49,115
All refs have to be rewritten and force-pushed,
tags included, because any ref into

295
00:21:49,155 --> 00:21:51,005
the old history keeps it alive.

296
00:21:51,345 --> 00:21:56,269
A rewrite adds objects and moves refs; old
objects remain until they are pruned, in

297
00:21:56,309 --> 00:22:00,456
every repository separately, and the
server keeps them after the push.

298
00:22:00,796 --> 00:22:05,456
A rewrite is for data that stays harmful
after rotation; for a key that was revoked

299
00:22:05,496 --> 00:22:07,892
within the hour it costs
more than it buys.

300
00:22:09,732 --> 00:22:18,169
Read sections 21B.16 and 21B.17 of the
textbook. Then do Exercise 31.4, Level 3,

301
00:22:18,209 --> 00:22:24,950
"Review this runbook", in
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

302
00:22:25,290 --> 00:22:30,143
Today you followed a rewrite from the fresh
clone to the forced push, and you saw

303
00:22:30,183 --> 00:22:33,586
what it leaves behind. Run the
lab once before you go on.

304
00:22:33,626 --> 00:22:38,660
Next time, a colleague who never read the
freeze message pulls and pushes: the stale

305
00:22:38,700 --> 00:22:43,553
clone that pushes the secret back, the
GitHub side of a rewrite, and the controls

306
00:22:43,593 --> 00:22:48,204
that limit blast radius. Until then,
look at the state first and type second.

307
00:22:48,244 --> 00:22:49,654
See you in the next one.
